      "width": 640,
      "height": 427,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Bob Jones",
      "description": "Trewavas Mine buildings The National Trust bought Trewavas Cliff and its iconic engine houses in February 2008 and work started almost immediately on a programme of renovation and repair. The buildings are a Scheduled Ancient Monument and form part of the Cornish Mining World Heritage Site.\nWheal Trewavas operated between 1834 and 1846, producing over 17,000 tonnes of copper and employing 200 people. It closed when the workings beneath the sea were flooded.\nThe restoration work being undertaken by local specialist contractors will replace rotten and missing lintels, as well as repairing serious structural faults and re-pointing the stonework using traditional lime mortar. Safety fencing around open mine shafts is also being improved.\n\nWork is scheduled to be completed by April 2009 and the cost, in excess of £500,000, has been funded by the Trust's Neptune Coastal Campaign, as well as a number of individual bequests.",
      "dateCreated": "2008-12-31",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-SA-2.0",
        "Images by Bob Jones",
        "Images from Geograph Britain and Ireland",
